3. Explain the process of deleting a node from a binary search tree in Python. 
Discuss how you would handle different cases, such as deleting a node with one, or two children. 
Additionally, explain any potential challenges or edge cases that may arise during the deletion 
process and how you would address them. 

When you try to delete a node from a binary search tree in python, the first thing you need to do is to find the
node that matches the number you are trying to delete. Once the number is found, there are different cases depending 
on how many children that specific node has. A node with no other children would just be deleted and that node would
be removed. If the node had one child, then the child node replaces the deleted node. Lastly, if there is a node 
with two children that needs to be deleted, then either the smallest value in the right subtree or the largest value
in the left subtree would replace the deleted node and that node would be deleted if it had no children. If that node
was taken from the largest number on the left and it had a left node child, then that child would replace the node 
that is replacing the deleted node. A potential challenge is if we couldn't find the node wanting to be deleted,
so I would just add try and except code and if the number is not found, then the program with still run.